1. Boosting Theory
Bagging vs Boosting
	Bagging (Random Forest)	Boosting (GBM, XGBoost)
Training	Parallel, independent trees	Sequential, each tree fixes the previous errors
Main goal	Reduce variance	Reduce bias (and some variance)
Base learner	Deep, low-bias trees	Shallow, weak trees (depth 3-8)
Overfitting risk	Low	Higher, needs regularization
Sensitivity to tuning	Low	High
AdaBoost intuition
Start with equal weights on all samples.
Train a weak learner, then increase weights of misclassified samples.
The next learner focuses on the hard cases.
Final prediction is a weighted vote of all learners.
Gradient Boosting

Build the model additively:

F_m(x) = F_{m-1}(x) + η · h_m(x)
h_m is a small tree fit to the pseudo-residuals, the negative gradient of the loss at the current prediction:
r_im = - ∂L(y_i, F(x_i)) / ∂F(x_i)   evaluated at F = F_{m-1}
For squared error, r = y - F(x), so each tree literally fits the residuals.
For log loss (classification), residuals are y - p, where p is the current predicted probability.
η is the learning rate (shrinkage).

Algorithm in 5 lines

Initialize with a constant (mean for regression, log-odds for classification).
Compute pseudo-residuals.
Fit a small tree to them.
Add the tree scaled by η.
Repeat for n_estimators rounds.
Key knobs and the trade-off
Parameter	Effect
learning_rate	Smaller = more robust, needs more trees
n_estimators	More trees = lower bias, risk of overfit
max_depth	Depth controls interaction order and complexity
subsample	Row sampling per tree (stochastic GB), reduces variance

Rule of thumb: learning_rate and n_estimators are coupled. Lower the rate (0.01-0.1) and let early stopping choose the number of trees.

Why boosting overfits

It keeps fitting residuals, including noise. Controls: shrinkage, subsampling, shallow trees, regularization, early stopping.

2. The Big Three Libraries
XGBoost
Regularized objective with second-order (gradient + Hessian) approximation:
Obj = Σ l(y_i, ŷ_i) + Σ Ω(f_k),    Ω(f) = γT + ½ λ Σ w_j²
Optimal leaf weight: w* = -G / (H + λ)
Split gain penalized by γ (minimum loss reduction to split).
Handles missing values by learning a default direction per split.
The benchmark standard, very stable.
LightGBM
Histogram binning: buckets continuous features, so it is fast and memory-light.
Leaf-wise growth (best-first) instead of level-wise: lower loss but can overfit small data. Control with num_leaves, min_child_samples.
GOSS (gradient-based one-side sampling) and EFB (exclusive feature bundling) for speed.
Native categorical support.
CatBoost
Ordered boosting and ordered target statistics reduce target leakage and prediction shift.
Best-in-class native categorical handling (no manual encoding).
Symmetric (oblivious) trees: fast inference, regularizing effect.
Strong defaults, so less tuning is needed.
When to choose which
Situation	Pick
General tabular benchmark, reliability	XGBoost
Large data (millions of rows), need speed	LightGBM
Many high-cardinality categorical features	CatBoost
Little time to tune	CatBoost
Small dataset	XGBoost / CatBoost (LightGBM can overfit)
Sklearn-compatible API
python
from xgboost import XGBClassifier
from lightgbm import LGBMClassifier
from catboost import CatBoostClassifier

xgb = XGBClassifier(n_estimators=500, learning_rate=0.05, max_depth=4,
                    eval_metric="aucpr", random_state=42)
lgbm = LGBMClassifier(n_estimators=500, learning_rate=0.05, num_leaves=31,
                      random_state=42, verbose=-1)
cat = CatBoostClassifier(iterations=500, learning_rate=0.05, depth=6,
                         random_seed=42, verbose=0)

Because they follow fit / predict / predict_proba, they drop straight into Pipeline, GridSearchCV, cross_val_score, and SHAP.

3. Handling Real-World Issues
Missing values
XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost handle NaN natively, so imputation is optional.
Sometimes native handling beats imputation because "missing" can itself be informative.
Linear models and SVMs still need imputation.
Imbalance
Library	Parameter
XGBoost	scale_pos_weight = n_negative / n_positive
LightGBM	is_unbalance=True or scale_pos_weight (not both)
CatBoost	auto_class_weights="Balanced" or class_weights=[...]
sklearn	class_weight="balanced"
python
ratio = (y_train == 0).sum() / (y_train == 1).sum()
xgb = XGBClassifier(scale_pos_weight=ratio)

Note: Both SMOTE and class weights shift predicted probabilities away from the true base rate. If you need real probabilities, recalibrate (Section 6).

Categorical features
Label/ordinal encoding works fine with tree models (no need for one-hot on high-cardinality columns).
Native support: LightGBM (categorical_feature, or pandas category dtype), CatBoost (cat_features=[...]), XGBoost (enable_categorical=True with tree_method="hist").
Avoid one-hot on high-cardinality columns (sparse, slow, weaker splits).
Monotonic constraints

Force the prediction to be non-decreasing/non-increasing in a feature. Valuable in credit and risk models for regulation and sanity (e.g., higher income should never raise default risk).

python
# 1 = increasing, -1 = decreasing, 0 = unconstrained (one entry per feature)
xgb = XGBClassifier(monotone_constraints=(1, -1, 0))
lgbm = LGBMClassifier(monotone_constraints=[1, -1, 0])
Using boosters inside the Day 9 pipeline
python
from imblearn.pipeline import Pipeline            # imblearn Pipeline, not sklearn, when using SMOTE
from imblearn.over_sampling import SMOTE
from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder
from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer

preprocess = ColumnTransformer([
    ("num", Pipeline([("imp", SimpleImputer(strategy="median")),
                      ("sc", StandardScaler())]), num_cols),
    ("cat", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown="ignore"), cat_cols),
])

pipe = Pipeline([
    ("prep", preprocess),
    ("smote", SMOTE(random_state=42)),     # applied ONLY inside training folds
    ("model", XGBClassifier(n_estimators=300, learning_rate=0.05)),
])

Scaling is unnecessary for tree models; it is only needed here if you also compare against linear models in the same pipeline.

4. Regularization & Early Stopping
Regularization parameters (XGBoost names)
Parameter	What it does	Typical range
reg_lambda (L2)	Shrinks leaf weights	0-10
reg_alpha (L1)	Sparsity in leaf weights	0-5
min_child_weight	Min Hessian sum per leaf, larger = more conservative	1-10
gamma	Min loss reduction to make a split	0-5
subsample	Row fraction per tree	0.6-1.0
colsample_bytree	Feature fraction per tree	0.5-1.0
max_depth	Tree depth	3-8

LightGBM equivalents: lambda_l2, lambda_l1, min_child_samples, min_split_gain, bagging_fraction, feature_fraction, num_leaves.

Early stopping

Stop adding trees when the validation metric has not improved for N rounds.

python
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split

X_tr, X_val, y_tr, y_val = train_test_split(
    X_train, y_train, test_size=0.2, stratify=y_train, random_state=42)

# XGBoost (recent versions: early_stopping_rounds goes in the constructor)
model = XGBClassifier(n_estimators=5000, learning_rate=0.03,
                      eval_metric="aucpr", early_stopping_rounds=50)
model.fit(X_tr, y_tr, eval_set=[(X_val, y_val)], verbose=False)
print(model.best_iteration)

# LightGBM (callbacks)
import lightgbm as lgb
lgbm = LGBMClassifier(n_estimators=5000, learning_rate=0.03)
lgbm.fit(X_tr, y_tr, eval_set=[(X_val, y_val)], eval_metric="average_precision",
         callbacks=[lgb.early_stopping(50), lgb.log_evaluation(0)])

Rules

Early-stop on a validation set, never the test set (that is leakage).
Set n_estimators high and let early stopping pick the right number.
With a pipeline, early stopping needs the eval set to be pre-transformed by the fitted preprocessor. Fit preprocessing on the training split, transform the validation split, then pass it as eval_set. (Do not SMOTE the validation set.)
Reading learning curves
python
results = model.evals_result()
# plot results["validation_0"]["aucpr"] vs iteration
Pattern	Diagnosis	Fix
Train and val both poor	Underfitting	More depth/trees, higher LR, less regularization
Train great, val plateaus then worsens	Overfitting	Lower depth, more regularization, subsample, early stop
Val still improving at the end	Not enough trees	More estimators, higher LR
5. Smarter Hyperparameter Tuning
Why GridSearch does not scale

Combinations grow exponentially (5 params x 5 values = 3,125 fits x CV folds). Most of the grid is wasted on unimportant regions.

RandomizedSearchCV

Samples random combinations and is usually just as good in far fewer fits (Bergstra & Bengio).

python
from sklearn.model_selection import RandomizedSearchCV, StratifiedKFold
from scipy.stats import randint, uniform, loguniform

param_dist = {
    "model__n_estimators": randint(200, 800),
    "model__learning_rate": loguniform(0.01, 0.3),
    "model__max_depth": randint(3, 9),
    "model__subsample": uniform(0.6, 0.4),
    "model__colsample_bytree": uniform(0.5, 0.5),
    "model__reg_lambda": loguniform(1e-2, 10),
}
search = RandomizedSearchCV(pipe, param_dist, n_iter=50, scoring="average_precision",
                            cv=StratifiedKFold(5, shuffle=True, random_state=42),
                            n_jobs=-1, random_state=42)
search.fit(X_train, y_train)
Bayesian optimization with Optuna
Builds a model of "which regions score well" and samples there (TPE sampler by default).
Pruning kills unpromising trials early.
Define the search space with trial.suggest_*.
python
import optuna
from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score

def objective(trial):
    params = {
        "n_estimators": trial.suggest_int("n_estimators", 200, 1000),
        "learning_rate": trial.suggest_float("learning_rate", 0.01, 0.3, log=True),
        "max_depth": trial.suggest_int("max_depth", 3, 9),
        "min_child_weight": trial.suggest_int("min_child_weight", 1, 10),
        "subsample": trial.suggest_float("subsample", 0.6, 1.0),
        "colsample_bytree": trial.suggest_float("colsample_bytree", 0.5, 1.0),
        "reg_lambda": trial.suggest_float("reg_lambda", 1e-2, 10, log=True),
        "reg_alpha": trial.suggest_float("reg_alpha", 1e-3, 5, log=True),
    }
    model = XGBClassifier(**params, eval_metric="aucpr", random_state=42, n_jobs=-1)
    pipe = Pipeline([("prep", preprocess), ("smote", SMOTE(random_state=42)),
                     ("model", model)])
    cv = StratifiedKFold(5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)
    return cross_val_score(pipe, X_train, y_train, cv=cv,
                           scoring="average_precision").mean()

study = optuna.create_study(direction="maximize",
                            sampler=optuna.samplers.TPESampler(seed=42),
                            pruner=optuna.pruners.MedianPruner())
study.optimize(objective, n_trials=60)
print(study.best_params, study.best_value)
Sensible tuning order
Fix learning rate (0.05-0.1) and find the number of trees with early stopping.
Tune tree structure: max_depth, min_child_weight (or num_leaves, min_child_samples).
Tune sampling: subsample, colsample_bytree.
Tune regularization: reg_lambda, reg_alpha, gamma.
Lower the learning rate (e.g., 0.01) and increase trees for the final model.
Nested CV for an honest estimate

Tuning on the same folds you report on gives an optimistic score. Nested CV separates the two:

Inner loop: hyperparameter search.
Outer loop: performance estimate of the whole tuning procedure.
python
inner = StratifiedKFold(3, shuffle=True, random_state=1)
outer = StratifiedKFold(5, shuffle=True, random_state=2)

search = RandomizedSearchCV(pipe, param_dist, n_iter=20, cv=inner,
                            scoring="average_precision", n_jobs=-1)
nested_scores = cross_val_score(search, X_train, y_train, cv=outer,
                                scoring="average_precision")
print(nested_scores.mean(), nested_scores.std())

It is expensive, so use it for the final comparison of models/procedures, not every experiment.

6. Probability Calibration
Probability accuracy vs label accuracy

A model can rank well (high ROC-AUC) yet output probabilities that mean nothing. If it says "0.8", about 80% of those cases should be positive. Calibration is the match between predicted probability and observed frequency.

Why it matters: risk scoring, expected-cost decisions, threshold tuning, combining model outputs, and communicating to stakeholders.

Who is miscalibrated?
Logistic regression: usually well calibrated.
Random forest: pushed away from 0 and 1 (under-confident at extremes).
Boosted trees: often distorted, especially with class weights, scale_pos_weight, or SMOTE.
SVM / Naive Bayes: typically poor.
Calibration curve (reliability diagram)

Bin predictions, then plot mean predicted probability (x) vs fraction of positives (y). The diagonal is perfect.

python
from sklearn.calibration import calibration_curve
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(y_val, p_val, n_bins=10, strategy="quantile")
plt.plot(prob_pred, prob_true, marker="o", label="model")
plt.plot([0, 1], [0, 1], "--", label="perfect")
plt.xlabel("Mean predicted probability"); plt.ylabel("Fraction of positives")
plt.legend(); plt.show()
Brier score

Mean squared error of probabilities (lower is better):

Brier = (1/N) Σ (p_i - y_i)²
python
from sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss
brier_score_loss(y_val, p_val)

Compare it with a baseline that always predicts the base rate. Log loss is another proper scoring rule.

Methods
Method	Idea	Best when
Platt scaling (sigmoid)	Fit a logistic regression on the model's scores	Small data, S-shaped distortion
Isotonic regression	Non-parametric monotonic step function	Larger data (roughly 1000+ calibration samples), more flexible but can overfit
CalibratedClassifierCV
python
from sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV

# Option 1: cross-validated calibration (fits on training data via internal CV)
cal = CalibratedClassifierCV(best_model, method="isotonic", cv=5)
cal.fit(X_train, y_train)

# Option 2: calibrate an already-fitted model on a held-out calibration set
from sklearn.frozen import FrozenEstimator   # sklearn >= 1.6
cal = CalibratedClassifierCV(FrozenEstimator(fitted_model), method="sigmoid")
cal.fit(X_calib, y_calib)

p_cal = cal.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]

(In older sklearn, cv="prefit" replaced FrozenEstimator.)

Rules

Calibrate on data the model was not trained on.
Evaluate calibration on a separate set (or via CV), not the calibration data.
Calibration changes probabilities, not ranking, so ROC-AUC barely changes. Brier and log loss improve.
Recalibrate before tuning the decision threshold, since thresholds are only meaningful on trustworthy probabilities.
With SMOTE/class weights, calibration restores probabilities consistent with the real-world base rate.
7. Common Mistakes
Mistake	Why it is wrong	Fix
Tuning learning_rate and n_estimators independently	They are coupled	Fix LR, early-stop to find trees, lower LR at the end
Early stopping on the test set	Leaks test information, inflated score	Use a validation split or CV
Blindly using default max_depth	Defaults may be far from optimal	Tune depth/leaves first
Trusting predict_proba from uncalibrated boosters	Probabilities may not be real risks	Check the reliability curve, calibrate
Comparing models with unequal tuning effort	Unfair comparison	Same CV, same trial budget
Skipping the logistic regression baseline	You cannot tell whether complexity helps	Always benchmark simple first
Wrong metric on imbalanced data	Accuracy hides failure	PR-AUC, recall at precision, F-beta
SMOTE before the train/val split	Synthetic leakage	SMOTE only inside the training fold (imblearn Pipeline)
Using num_leaves > 2^max_depth in LightGBM	Overfitting	Keep num_leaves < 2^max_depth
Using both is_unbalance and scale_pos_weight	Double weighting	Use only one
